```todo
Goal: Refactor VGE shader stack (graphics + compute) to:
- Put all non-opaque shader parameters into std140 UBOs (no standalone float/vec/mat uniforms).
- Use explicit binding locations for SPIR-V (UBOs/SSBOs/images/samplers) so bindings are deterministic.
- Make `GpuProgramLayout` the single source of truth for resource contracts (bindings/units/names).
- Ensure attempts to set unused/optimized-away resources never issue GL calls (warn at most, then abort).

Non-goals / constraints:
- GLSL limitation: sampler/image (opaque) uniforms cannot be members of UBOs. For those, the plan is explicit resource bindings (preferred) or link-time sampler-unit assignment with reflection fallback.
  Optional future: bindless textures (GL_ARB_bindless_texture) to make handles data-driven via buffers.

References:
- UBOs + std140 layout: https://registry.khronos.org/OpenGL/specs/gl/glspec46.core.pdf (Uniform Blocks / Standard Uniform Block Layout)
- std140 packing cheatsheet (Khronos wiki): https://www.khronos.org/opengl/wiki/Interface_Block_(GLSL)#Standard_uniform_block_layout
- glUniformBlockBinding: https://registry.khronos.org/OpenGL-Refpages/gl4/html/glUniformBlockBinding.xhtml
- Program interface queries (active resources): https://registry.khronos.org/OpenGL-Refpages/gl4/html/glGetProgramResourceiv.xhtml
- Explicit attrib locations extension (vertex attributes): https://registry.khronos.org/OpenGL/extensions/ARB/ARB_explicit_attrib_location.txt
- 420-pack (layout(binding=...) in older GLSL): https://registry.khronos.org/OpenGL/extensions/ARB/ARB_shading_language_420pack.txt
- SPIR-V in OpenGL (ARB_gl_spirv): https://registry.khronos.org/OpenGL/extensions/ARB/ARB_gl_spirv.txt
- Repo: docs/OpenGL.SPIR-V.BuildAndRuntime.todo, docs/GpuProgram.md, docs/OpenGL.PipelineStateObject.Overview.md

✔ Phase 0 — Binding Registry + Conventions
  Exit artifacts: binding tables exist; naming is stable; everyone agrees what “UBO-only” means.
  ✔ Reserve binding points for common blocks (example):
    - Frame/View UBO (per-frame constants)
    - Object UBO (per-draw / per-dispatch constants)
    - Material UBO (material params)
    - Lights UBO (dynamic light lists)
  ✔ Reserve binding ranges for compute-only resources:
    - SSBO bindings
    - image units
  ✔ Reserve texture-unit ranges per pass (graphics) and per pipeline (compute) for fallbacks.
  ✔ Document engine attribute indices and enforce vertex shaders using:
    - `#extension GL_ARB_explicit_attrib_location: enable`
  ✔ Decide SPIR-V GLSL version baseline for VGE shaders (recommended: `#version 460 core` for SPIR-V path).

✔ Phase 1 — Shader Interface Migration: “UBO-only” (Non-opaque)
  Exit artifacts: no standalone float/vec/mat uniforms remain; parameters are grouped into UBOs.
  ✔ Define std140 block schemas in shared includes (VGE `@import`):
    - Frame/View block (matrices, fog, time, camera)
    - Object block (model/chunk transforms, draw flags)
    - Material block (tints/PBR knobs)
    - Lights block (counts + vec4 arrays)
  ✔ Add explicit UBO bindings for SPIR-V builds:
    - `layout(std140, binding = N) uniform <BlockName> { ... };
  ✔ Remove “set-every-uniform-every-frame” style for non-opaque values:
    - All those values must come from UBOs.
  ✔ Create a compatibility macro layer (temporary):
    - Legacy uniform names become macro aliases to UBO fields to reduce churn.

✔ Phase 2 — `GpuProgramLayout` as the Single Source of Truth
  Exit artifacts: every program/pipeline has an associated layout object that knows bindings and can apply/validate them.
  ✔ Extend docs for the contract concept and responsibilities.
  ✔ Add a shader-specific subclass pattern:
    - `MyShaderLayout : GpuProgramLayout`
    - Contains strongly-typed accessors for resources (UBO handles, textures, images, SSBOs).
  ✔ Implement a unified “ApplyContract(program)” entrypoint:
    - Applies UBO binding points, SSBO bindings, image bindings, and (if needed) sampler-unit uniforms.
  ✔ Make `GpuProgramLayout` the only place allowed to:
    - Resolve bindings by name (reflection)
    - Cache resolved locations/indices
    - Decide whether a resource is optional/required

☐ Phase 3 — Explicit Binding Support Matrix + Fallback Policy
  Exit artifacts: behavior is deterministic on modern GL; graceful degradation on limited drivers.
  ☐ Define capability checks (via `GpuSupport`) that determine when to use:
    - Hardcoded explicit bindings (preferred)
    - Reflection-based resolution and binding (fallback)
  ☐ For GLSL fallback paths that cannot compile `layout(binding=...)`:
    - Use `glUniformBlockBinding` after link based on layout contract.
  ☐ For samplers (opaque uniforms):
    - Preferred: explicit binding decorations in SPIR-V / GLSL where supported.
    - Fallback: set sampler uniforms to fixed units once after link (never per-frame).

☐ Phase 4 — Unused / Optimized-away Resource Guards (No GL Errors)
  Exit artifacts: attempting to set an inactive resource is a no-op with optional warn-once logging.
  ☐ Define a tri-state for each resource name:
    - Resolved & active
    - Missing/inactive (optimized away)
    - Reflection not supported / unknown
  ☐ Add guard rails for each operation:
    - Uniform location `-1` → abort set (warn once)
    - Uniform block index invalid (`GL_INVALID_INDEX`) → abort bind (warn once)
    - Program resource not active (via program interface query) → abort (warn once)
  ☐ Add an “optional resource” marker to suppress warnings for feature-flagged shaders.
  ☐ Add lightweight diagnostics counters (dev-only): attempted sets skipped due to inactive.

☐ Phase 5 — Integrate Layouts into Graphics (`GpuProgram`) Lifecycle
  Exit artifacts: graphics programs apply layout contracts on link/relink and expose only layout-driven accessors.
  ☐ Ensure `GpuProgram`:
    - Owns/attaches a `GpuProgramLayout`
    - Calls `layout.ApplyContract(...)` after successful link and after any recompile
  ☐ Refactor existing per-shader wrappers (LumOn, PBR, debug, etc):
    - Move uniform/texture/UBO property accessors into layout subclasses
    - Wrapper becomes thin: create program, attach layout, set defines, bind resources

☐ Phase 6 — Integrate Layouts into Compute (`GpuComputePipeline`) Lifecycle
  Exit artifacts: compute pipelines use the same contract application + inactive guards.
  ☐ Make compute pipelines accept a `GpuProgramLayout` (or a compute-specialized subclass).
  ☐ Replace compute’s ad-hoc uniform-location cache with layout resolution + guards.
  ☐ Ensure resource binding for:
    - UBOs (frame/object)
    - SSBOs (buffers)
    - images (rw textures)
    - samplers (if any)

☐ Phase 7 — Runtime Update Strategy: UBO Uploads Instead of `glUniform*` Storm
  Exit artifacts: per-frame constants updated once; per-draw/per-dispatch uses ranges/ring buffers; fewer driver calls.
  ☐ Implement/update UBO upload strategy:
    - Preferred: persistent mapped ring buffer (if supported)
    - Fallback: orphan + subdata or map-range invalidate
  ☐ Split update cadence:
    - Frame/View UBO: once per frame
    - Object/Material UBO: per draw/dispatch (ideally bind-range)
  ☐ Confirm correctness across shader recompiles and feature toggles.

☐ Phase 8 — Validation, Tooling, and Cleanup
  Exit artifacts: regression risk is managed; old uniform paths removed.
  ☐ Add a dev-mode validation pass:
    - Compare expected contract vs linked program reflection results
    - Log mismatches with shader/program identifiers
  ☐ Add documentation:
    - How to add a new shader: create layout subclass + UBO schema
    - Binding registry reference
    - Rules: “no standalone non-opaque uniforms”
  ☐ Remove deprecated uniform-setting helpers once migration is complete.
  ☐ Optional: investigate bindless textures as a separate phase (capability gated).
```